Reflow keeps a wide glyph whole, and drops the gap it left

Reflow cut a line every new_cols cells, so a double-width glyph whose
first half fell in the last column was split across two rows: one cell
of it at the edge and a blank starting the next. And the empty cell a
wide glyph leaves at the edge when it wraps -- by the program's output
or by an earlier reflow -- was counted as part of the line, so joining
the rows again put a space into the text.

Walk each line as a list of its cells rather than by row arithmetic,
leaving out an empty last cell before a row that starts with a wide
glyph, and end a row one cell early where the next cell would be the
far half of a wide glyph. Line heights are counted the same way, so the
rows a line needs and the rows it gets agree.

